INT. MOONLIGHT LANES BOWLING ALLEY - LEAGUE FINALS - NIGHT

A cavernous alley. Neon pins glow over lane eleven. Rows of folding chairs hold a thinning crowd. A scoreboard reads 188 to 211.

COACH MAGS DELACROIX, 50s, in a satin team jacket, paces behind the approach with a clipboard she hasn't looked at. TY, 25, the anchor, chalks his hands. PAOLO, 19, a rookie in rented shoes, stares at the pins as though they've said something rude.

A STRANGER, 40s, in a camel coat, sits two rows up with a notebook. He's been there since the fourth frame.

MAGS
Ty, you're fine. Paolo, you're up.

PAOLO
I thought Ty was up.

TY
I'm not up.

MAGS
You're not up, he's up.

PAOLO
I'm not ready.

TY
You're ready.

MAGS
You're ready.

PAOLO
I'm so not ready.

All three talk at once, an overlapping chorus that goes nowhere. Paolo steps to the line anyway. He lets the ball go, and it sails into the gutter with an apologetic thump.

The crowd groans. Ty closes his eyes. Mags writes nothing on the clipboard.

The Stranger closes his notebook, stands, and walks down the aisle. He stops at the rail.

STRANGER
Coach Delacroix. A word.

MAGS
After the game.

STRANGER
It's already after.

She looks at the scoreboard, then at him.

MAGS
Who are you?

FELIX
Felix Landry. Regional development. I've been watching your kid since the second week.

PAOLO
Mine?

FELIX
You. Not the one in the gutter, the one who stayed.

TY
He stayed?

FELIX
He's been here every night after close. Alone. Rolling the ball into the left gutter, over and over, trying to find where it bends.

Mags turns to Paolo. He has gone red to the ears.

PAOLO
It bends at the seventh board.

MAGS
You knew that?

PAOLO
I thought it was cheating.

FELIX
It's not cheating. It's called oil.

TY
He wasn't supposed to tell you that.

FELIX
I'm not here to tell. I'm here to offer. We take two to the state qualifier. I only need one of you to say yes.

Ty steps up, chalk-white hands open.

TY
Take him.

PAOLO
What?

TY
Take him. He's got something. I'm just loud.

The scoreboard blinks. Mags sets the clipboard on a chair and, for the first time all night, smiles.
